From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Date: Sat, 2 Apr 94 6:33:10 WET
Subject: [#94-4-15] Simulation vs. Reality - part 2: sentient machines

Stephen J. Whitrow writes:
> The mechanists have got two choices [and I don't think many would go for
> 1) these days]: 
> 
> 1)  They can adopt the philosophical "barefoot" behaviourism, popular until
> about the fifties / sixties (particularly with the Soviets).  If men are
> essentially just unconscious automata, whose output can be consistently
                   ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
> predicted from their sensory inputs, it's quite likely that simulation of
> their particles on a machine would successfully simulate each individual.
> After all, the simulation needn't be conscious.  However, this leads to a
> form of solipsism -- it can't be applied to oneself (except for people who
> claim to be zombies), and one's own mental states still exist in an
> unexplained mental world.  (Whether or not this is technically a fallacy of
> self-exclusion, it sure sounds like it!)
 
  I don't understand why Soviets have to be brought into the
analogy unless you are trying to make mechanism sound scary. Anyway,
the underlined phrase zeros in on why your proposed "either-or" is flawed.
You have already assumed that automata can not be conscious which is
not proven (you can't prove it by philosophy alone) However, in
a previous paragraph you talked about nondeterminism being a useful part of 
your ideal theory, but even nondeterminism can be modeled by automata.
In the case of finite machines, every nondeterministic finite automaton
can be converted to a deterministic finite automaton. Now, you might
argue that brains have more than finite precision which is a possibility,
but probably not all of that precision is useful information. On the
other hand, the brain can not store more than a finite amount of information.
Recently, a thread on the cypherpunks list brought up the Bekenstein
bound which (I hope I am interpreting it correctly) states that
in any region of space enclosed by a certain surface area, only a finite
amount of distinguishable states may be stored (and I assume that
attempting to store more leads to a black hole) Quantum gravity
or some more precise theory might change this but I think it's safe to
assume that the brain can only store a finite amount of information.
The thing which has to be decided is that it only does a finite calculation
between each state.

[much deleted. I don't understand how you are defining consciousness.
"heat differences?" never heard that one before]

> Ironically, once you move too far from the identity theory of tying up
> each possible mental state with a specific physical state, yet don't
> revert to behaviourism, this leads towards a mild dualism.  You have to
> assume that an individual's mental states mysteriously arise just through
> having an accurate enough algorithm, and it's not supposed to matter
> whether buttons or ball-cocks and cisterns are used to run the algorithm.
> Each mental state does not tie up with a corresponding physical state,
> only a logical state.  Therefore qualia cannot be reduced to physical
> states, hence these physical states are independent of the "mind".

   It's hard to follow this, but I think the error you are making is
the same one which prevents you from imagining that microscopic
phenomena are responsible for macroscopic thermodynamics, or the same
one which stops creationists from imagining how evolution could have
produced beings as fantastically complex as us. The error is that
seemingly simple phenonmena cannot lead *in combination* over time or space
to very complex phenomena. (i.e. that any combination of algorithms
could produce something as complex as the mind) Also, logical states and 
physical states are convertable (give me a physical state, and I'll
give you a machine with the same logical state, or I'll convert a
logical state into a physical device representing that logical state)

> In my alternative, each possible mental state, however complex, could
> be represented by its own particular landscape involving the fifth
> dimension of electromagnetic space.  Let's say you're conceiving of a group
> of people living in a muddy commune, one section stocked up with Molotov
> cocktails for the revolution, another with booze and crack for their
> recreation.  Each concept would have its particular meme or neuronal
> structure; the total mental state would be described by all of these
> firing, maybe with various spiking frequencies or amplitudes to define the
> relative intensity of each element.  The full result would be a complex set
> of e.m. waves relating to this mental state, perpendicular to the world-
> line of the individual in the volume of space occupied by the brain.  With
> electromagnetic waves explained as ripples in the fifth dimension, a given
> mental state would be represented by a set of fifth-dimension warps along
> a section of the individual's world-line approximately 200-400 mS in
> length.  Since stronger signals with a longer range such as mains hum
> would attenuate less sharply across the individual's world-line compared
> to true signals emanating from neurons and would not correspond to any
> learned memetic signal, they would be rejected as meaningless noise.

  Nice theory (sounds almost like Star Trek technobabble), but Fifth dimension?
Doesn't exist except in Kaluza-Klien models which aren't generally accepted. 
Still, it doesn't matter how many dimensions you add, your EM waves will still
be predictable by a model because they can be described by a theory
(indeed, you give one). What you must exibit to prevent a UTM from
being able to simulate your system is a system which does more than a
finite amount of labor at each step (and thus invalidate church's thesis)
The interaction of fields are particles in your theory must not be
predictable (i.e. they must be totally random) and the system as
a whole must compute more than a TM.
 
> If consciousness requires _anything_ other than a succession of logical
> states, and the universe is not a simulation, then consciousness could not
> be simulated since a simulation of the particles would not reproduce the
> necessary physical conditions.  This would even apply if consciousness has
> no effects on behaviour; the simulation would behave properly but would be
> unconscious or its subjective experiences would be inaccurate.

   For consciousness or the universe not to be a sucession of
*causually related* states (you can define a state for any system, and 
therefore derive a sucession of them) the universe would have to be
non-linear and non-causual. That is, there can be no information
derived for a future state from past states (similar to a totally
random bit stream, the next bit can not be predicted from any former
bits)
   You for looking for a strong version of free will which I don't
think exists. I think our thoughts are contrained by physical law,
and the state of our brain at any given moment. That doesn't
neccessarily mean that they are simply deterministic (there's probably a 
random element), but having your thoughts controlled by random 
non-determinism is no more "free" than having it controled by a 
pseudo-random deterministic generator.

> If the universe is a simulation then our consciousness is likely just a
> succession of logical states.  But then it might be said that the simulated
> beings were already simulations, therefore they hadn't successfully 
> simulated themselves even if they thought they had -- it was an illusion.  

   Why do you believe a simulation of a simulation is not a simulation,
or that simulations are illusions? You are making arguments by 
assuming the conclusion. You assume that simulated things are
illusory in the context of the simulation, you assume that
automata are unconscious or unliving, and you assume that you have
more free will than you actually have.
   Assume this universe *IS* a simulation. To you, simulated things
are real because you are in the simulation. There is no experiment
or argument you can perform to detect whether or not this is all
simulated. To you this is not an illusion because this is the only
reality you have ever known. (similar to a Star Trek holodeck
character)

-Ray



