From: goodwin@snug.demon.co.uk (Tim Goodwin)
Date: Sat, 02 Apr 94 12:12:25 GMT
Subject: [#94-4-16] Trouble with Left Anarchists

In message <940402091632_74204.3113_BHT21-1@CompuServe.COM>
 "James H. Daugherty" writes:
> 
> Left anarchists, in my opinion, are hopelessly tied to non-sensical
> ideas of spontaneous socialism and would fight as a mob to
> exproppriate anything extropians or other believers in property
> tried to build (especially if we were so evil as to offer
> employment to left anarchist drifters instead of opening the
> Board of Directors to all of them that happened by!).
> 
> Without secure property rights no one bothers to work for the
> future. . .just like left anarchists.
> 
> The fantasy about "free land" above to which I am responding
> is total nonsense.  To use land you must be able to lay claim to
> it in some fashion. . .either first use, renting, or purchasing.
> I won't negotiate with the local mob every time I want to make a
> productive decision on my land.
> 

I find your assertion that spontaneous socialism is non-sensical somewhat 
specious when spontaneous order, an Extropian basic tenet, is deemed to be 
possible.  Surely socialism, whether you like it or not, is a form of order and 
can thus be assumed to be theoretically spontaneously possible.

"Left anarchist drifters" seems to me to be a perjorative term that encourages 
stereotyping and offers little in the way of rational debate.

"Free land" is of course a fantasy.  It is something that people, not 
necessarily left anarchists,have been fighting for for years.  From the days of 
the enclosures, when common land was taken forcibly into private ownership and 
was opposed by groups like the Diggers and their ill-fated stand on St Georges 
Hill, the concept of private ownership of land has been a political hot potato.

It seems to me that what is lacking in this debate is any justification for the 
private ownership of land.  Note: I am not talking about use of land or defence 
of the use of it. I am talking about ownership.

In the UK large tracts of land that were in common use are privately owned.  
It  has not been until fairly recently that landlords offered a public right 
of way through miles and miles of some of the most beautiful moorland and hills 
in the country.  Again, this right did not come about through the enlightened 
attitude of the land owners, but mass campaigns and mass trespasses through the 
land in question.

What on earth is the justification for ownership of part of the planet that we 
were all born on?  The Diggers manifesto 1649: "No man has any right to buy and 
sell the earth for private gain."  I still happen to think that this is not a 
bad idea.  Soon the air we breathe will be up for sale.

