From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Sat, 02 Apr 94 15:51:01 GMT
Subject: [#94-4-17] PCR: Relative Boundary of Certainty

On 27 Mar 94 16:17:32 EST, Reilly Jones wrote:
>The maybe-I-am-a-simulation possibility I have never seen as more than
>sophistry, easily eliminatable with reflection over time on the richness of
>sensory experience and the free will of thought to choose subjective purposes
>and then act on those purposes.

I don't see simulation as a credible reality; however, it does seem just
possible that it could be proven true if I suddenly wake up in the
simulators' reality, and they explain to me how it was all done.  The
arguments from introspection -- richness of sensory experience, free will
of thought, etc -- look rather good to me, but I'd admit I could be
mistaken.  So I'd give simulation a probability of about 10^-20, whilst
being certain in my belief that "I am not a simulation" is an extremely
credible belief.

>Steve Whitrow again: <But how could you be certain that my posts aren't written
>jointly by five people together with an AI program, and that "Steve Whitrow" is
>just a pseudonym?  It might even be the name given to the AI, which actually
>favours entropic death worship, but keeps getting overruled by the humans.  You
>could be certain that you believe this to be unlikely, but you couldn't be
>certain of its certainty in objective reality due to the epistemological
>limits.>
>
>I'm nervous enough about espousing ideas that directly challenge very powerful
>groups of people world-wide without being made more nervous wondering about
>entropic death-worshipping covert AIs subverting this list.  Thank you very
>much.

Sorry if the above was startling; covert operations to infiltrate the list
by entropic death-worshippers couldn't be ruled out, but I don't think we
should allow this to silence us.  I'm certain that I couldn't possibly be
mistaken over my belief that I'm opposed to entropic death-worship
(although it's possible that over periods of a fraction of a second I
might be mistaken over perceived images, for instance).  And for any
beliefs that I'm almost certain of (maybe assigning them a probability of
0.9999999999 for example) I'd act as if they were true.  

Ideally you would evaluate all possibilities, and take into account their
probabilities and the risk/reward ratio of each, to maximise utility.  If
we ever decided that the probability of corruption by entropists times the
probability of their harming our interests times the stakes involved were
too high, it would then be more profitable for us to infiltrate the
entropists.  Or if we were confident that consensual reality could forge
consensual purpose but third parties represented a serious threat, we
could use more secure communication channels.
 
By the way, if there are two opposing theories, one believed by 99% of
people and another believed by 1% of people, and I believe the more
popular theory, I'd expect this to make the popular theory consensual
reality (from my viewpoint) since it's "we".  But the alternative's being
"they" doesn't make it objective reality, so it doesn't seem that "they"
is always a good term for objective reality.  I'd have objective reality
as slightly more likely to be the theory with 99% support, although you'd
have to judge each on their own merits. 

