From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Sat, 02 Apr 94 19:58:17 GMT
Subject: [#94-4-18] RIGHTS: Invisible Hand

Reilly Jones wrote on 27 Mar 94 16:17:01 EST
(re my matrix on subj./cons./obj. reality vs. nat. disorder/order/design):

>I have played around with matrices like this but mine get complicated quickly
>because I throw in memetic evolution as a much more complex selectional system.
>This more complex selectional system is what makes for a mixed economy like
>your milk price example.  Your matrix is a good representation of what really
>happens.  You could add natural language, semiotics, pragmatics into c.r./s.o.
>and artificial languages, mathematics and logic into c.r./design.  You could
>add survival values into s.r./s.o. and chosen values under s.r./design.

I'd have laws of maths and logic -- at least, a number of them -- as
objective reality and possibly spontaneous orders -- discovered rather than
designed.  I believe them to be pre-existing orders with an objective
existence, "written into the universe" if you like.  Although unlike the
typical spontaneous order (e.g. a dinosaur brain), they didn't evolve into
existence over a time, but either appeared instantly (Big Bang cosmologies)
or are eternal (ageless infinite universe cosmologies).  I don't think that
should disqualify them from the spontaneous order category, unless we add a
slot in for pre-existing orders of nature.  

Another thing that might fit into the same category is the utility of
rights, although the rights themselves would be part of consensual reality.

>I've been trying to model how a university should be organized around the
>different specialties of knowledge and where interdepartmental efforts are
>crucial to gain broader understanding of the larger framework that memetic
>evolution fits within.

This sounds like the sort of operation that would benefit memetic
evolution.

