From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Date: Sat, 2 Apr 94 19:26:20 WET
Subject: [#94-4-19] Trouble with Left Anarchists

[suggestion: cut your lines to 78 columns in the future]
Tim Goodwin writes:
> I find your assertion that spontaneous socialism is non-sensical somewhat 
> specious when spontaneous order, an Extropian basic tenet, is deemed to be 
> possible.  Surely socialism, whether you like it or not, is a form of order and 
> can thus be assumed to be theoretically spontaneously possible.

  The usual Extropian viewpoint is that socialism is inherently
unstable.

> It seems to me that what is lacking in this debate is any justification for the 
> private ownership of land.  Note: I am not talking about use of land or defence 
> of the use of it. I am talking about ownership.

   Before it was private land, it was unowned. The first person to 
possess the land, and defend it, has ownership of it. I don't support the
claim that before property was owned, it was owned by everyone.

> What on earth is the justification for ownership of part of the planet that we 
> were all born on?  The Diggers manifesto 1649: "No man has any right to buy and 
> sell the earth for private gain."  I still happen to think that this is not a 
> bad idea.  Soon the air we breathe will be up for sale.

  I think it is a terrible idea because it is totally unworkable as a
system for allocating land resources. The idea that land use could be
democratically (without State!) allocated efficiently is a fantasy.
Local mobs who "democratically" decide to do something to local land
merely establish a defacto socialist state with those who were out-
voted being forced to watch land being used for something they
didn't want. All this does is create a political class where the mob
votes what it wants and dissenters get the shaft. This is not any
better than a property system and in fact it is much worse because
the minority has no recourse (like in a market) and it is incredibility
bureaucratic.
  The only way the Diggers philosophy would work is on a frontier where
population is sparse and land is plentiful, and the major product was
agriculture. This isn't the situation today.





